    isbefore: []  # ebe1
    duplicate_of: []  # ebe1
    not_duplicate_of: []  # ebe1
    pr_ref:  # ebe1
    description: "## Statement\n\nFramework detection MUST detect autoload-by-convention frameworks (Rails, Phoenix, Spring Boot, Laravel, etc.) even when BOTH (a) the manifest is silent (no Gemfile / `pom.xml` / `mix.exs` declaration) AND (b) no import edges to the framework exist (because the framework is autoloaded at runtime, not imported in source code), provided (c) the repo carries characteristic file-structure conventions that uniquely identify the framework.\n\nThis is a strictly weaker fallback than manifest detection and import-edge promotion (WI-palol / WI-pusad). The expected hit rate on real repos is small — most real-world Rails apps have `gem 'rails'` in the Gemfile (handled by WI-lohok's `_AUTOLOAD_BY_CONVENTION_FRAMEWORKS` exemption); most real-world Phoenix apps have `{:phoenix, ...}` in `mix.exs`. The case this INV addresses is the residue: fork-from-template repos where the manifest was scrubbed, internal forks of framework code (a Rails monorepo housing Rails itself, where Rails-isn't-a-dep-because-Rails-IS-the-repo), and pathological CI artifacts.\n\n## Why this is distinct from INV-rojip\n\nINV-rojip's structural fix is **promote frameworks from import edges** — it consumes the same edge data already in the IR. WI-palol and WI-pusad implement two arms of that approach (specific-pattern promotion and bare-name-with-compound-submodule promotion).\n\nThis INV addresses a case where NEITHER signal exists: the manifest is silent AND no import edges to the framework exist. The only signal left is **repo file structure** — directory layout, magic filenames, characteristic content patterns inside specific files. That's a different mechanism class entirely; it requires opening files the analyzers may not have visited (because they have no matching language extensions or are excluded by convention), pattern-matching their content, and producing a framework-detection signal without crossing into full analyzer territory.\n\nFiling this separately preserves INV-rojip's scope as \"use the data plumbing we already have\" and prevents WI-pusad from sprawling into \"and also implement file-structure heuristics across N frameworks.\"\n\n## Motivating cohort: chatwoot Rails-without-Gemfile\n\nINV-rojip's closure cohort 3 (chatwoot Rails) is mostly handled by WI-lohok's autoload exemption when Rails IS declared in the Gemfile. The remaining residue:\n\n- A repo where Rails IS used (real `app/controllers/`, real `config/routes.rb`, real `Rakefile` with `Rails::Application.load_tasks`), but the Gemfile is missing, scrubbed, or doesn't declare `rails`.\n- No source file says `require 'rails'` — Bundler autoloads it; lacking the Bundler declaration, even that signal is absent.\n- Hypergumbo today reports `frameworks=[]`; rails.yaml never loads; controller/route/form/serializer concept linkers starve.\n\nWI-pusad's compound-submodule gate doesn't help: there are no Rails imports at all in app code. WI-lohok's exemption requires Rails to be IN `profile.frameworks` initially (manifest detection) before the skip-demote logic applies.\n\n## Proposed mechanism (sketch — not the WI's burden to design fully)\n\nPer-framework, declare a **file-structure signature**: a small set of (path-glob, content-pattern) tuples that, if jointly satisfied, are pathognomonic for the framework. Examples:\n\n- **Rails:** `config/application.rb` AND grep `Rails::Application` in that file. (`Rakefile` with `Rails::Application.load_tasks` is a backup; `app/controllers/application_controller.rb` is too generic — Sinatra apps and home-grown MVCs use the same path.)\n- **Phoenix:** `mix.exs` containing `:phoenix` (manifest-adjacent but inside the file body, not just the deps list) AND `lib/*_web/router.ex` containing `Phoenix.Router` use directive.\n- **Spring Boot:** any `*.java` containing the `@SpringBootApplication` annotation (already detected via analyzer Symbol kind; the WI here is wiring that signal back to framework detection).\n- **Laravel:** `artisan` AND `config/app.php` containing `Laravel\\Framework`.\n\nDetection runs AFTER manifest scanning and AFTER import-edge promotion (WI-palol / WI-pusad), as a third-and-last-resort path. The file-structure check is gated on the framework's languages being present in `profile.languages` to avoid false-positive Rails detection on a pure-Python repo with an accidental `config/application.rb`.\n\nStorage: the signatures live as a new module-level dict, conceptually `FRAMEWORK_FILE_STRUCTURE_SIGNATURES`, in `profile.py` or a dedicated `frameworks/file_structure.py`. Per ADR-0024 §\"use judgment\" carveout, the catalog could be code or YAML; lean toward YAML so the signatures are reviewable by humans without grokking Python.\n\n## Closure criterion\n\nThis INV closes `satisfied` when:\n\n1. `refine_frameworks` (or a successor pass) detects at least one autoload-by-convention framework via file-structure signature when the manifest is silent AND no import edges exist.\n2. A bakeoff cohort confirms:\n   - **Chatwoot-without-Gemfile** (synthetic — delete the Gemfile and re-run): Rails framework detected via file-structure signature. routes.txt / controllers / forms / serializers populate from rails.yaml.\n   - **Hypergumbo self-analysis:** no false-positive Rails / Phoenix / Spring Boot detection on a Python-only codebase.\n   - **A real Phoenix repo where `mix.exs` declares phoenix:** no double-detection collision between manifest and file-structure paths.\n3. The mechanism does NOT regress WI-palol's specific-pattern promotion or WI-pusad's compound-submodule bare-name promotion.\n\n## Design tensions to resolve in implementing WI\n\n1. **Signature ordering / precedence.** When does file-structure detection fire? Strictly after manifest + promote, as a fallback? Or in parallel, with manifest+import precedence in case of conflict? The fallback ordering is simpler but may miss cases where manifest detection over-counts (e.g., a Gemfile with `gem 'rails'` in a non-Rails sub-repo). Parallel detection with conflict resolution is more general.\n2. **Confidence labeling.** A framework detected only by file-structure signature should arguably carry a lower confidence than one detected by manifest. Surface this on `RepoProfile` somehow (new field? `dev_frameworks` is the wrong bucket — dev means dev-only, not low-confidence). Or do all `profile.frameworks` entries carry equal weight and downstream consumers don't care?\n3. **Catalog curation tax.** WI-palol was explicitly designed to ESCAPE the per-language curation tax (each new ecosystem cost a targeted patch). File-structure signatures reintroduce that tax: each new framework needs its own signature. Is the tax worth paying for the small residue this INV addresses? The answer is probably \"yes for the top 5-10 autoload frameworks (Rails, Phoenix, Spring Boot, Laravel, ASP.NET MVC), no for the long tail\" — which means the WI under this INV has a built-in scope bound and the INV's closure doesn't require coverage of every autoload framework.\n4. **File-content reading cost.** The proposed signatures read file contents (grep `Rails::Application` in `config/application.rb`). For each candidate framework, the worst-case scan opens up to a few small files. Acceptable cost given the small expected hit rate; verify with a perf test on a large repo (e.g., wasmtime, vscode) that file-structure detection adds <1s to wall-clock time.\n5. **Interaction with WI-lohok's `_AUTOLOAD_BY_CONVENTION_FRAMEWORKS` set.** WI-lohok's set members skip the demote check entirely. If a framework is detected ONLY by file-structure signature, it should arguably also skip the demote check (no import edges to demote against). The simplest composition: file-structure-detected frameworks join the autoload set implicitly. Alternative: keep them separate so future logic can distinguish \"manifest-confirmed + autoload exemption\" from \"file-structure-only inference.\"\n\n## Why P4\n\nThis INV addresses a residue of a residue. Manifest detection covers the common case. WI-palol covers workspace-import scopes. WI-pusad covers bare-name compound-submodule imports. WI-lohok covers manifest-declared autoload frameworks. Each layer catches the obvious cases; what's left for this INV is the genuinely rare repo where ALL of those fail.\n\nThe expected hit rate is small — handful of repos in the prospector corpus, not a structural issue affecting most analysis. P4 reflects that. The mechanism's design cost is also non-trivial (per-framework signatures, content-pattern matching, ordering vs. existing paths) so the cost/benefit pencils out to \"file it, defer it, work it when bakeoff data justifies the priority.\"\n\n## Spec / ADR compatibility\n\n- `docs/hypergumbo-spec.md` §profile (line 758): unchanged. Detection methodology not constrained.\n- No ADR governs framework-detection methodology. A new ADR may be warranted if/when the file-structure mechanism lands — the three detection paths (manifest, import-edge promotion, file-structure) together constitute a load-bearing design decision worth recording.\n\n## Related (non-blocking)\n\n- **INV-rojip** (violated) — Parent class of \"framework detection from imports.\" This INV is the file-structure complement; the two together cover all three detection paths.\n- **WI-palol** (done) — Promote phase, specific patterns. Sibling.\n- **WI-pusad** (todo_hard) — Promote phase, bare-name patterns. Sibling.\n- **WI-lohok** (done) — `_AUTOLOAD_BY_CONVENTION_FRAMEWORKS` skip-demote exemption. Adjacent — the file-structure mechanism may extend or compose with this exemption set.\n- **WI-himas** (done) — Layered manifest scanner. Adjacent — different signal source.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- **Frameworks with no characteristic file-structure signature.** Some frameworks (React, Express, Flask used as a CLI) have no pathognomonic file layout. They're covered by manifest detection or by WI-palol/WI-pusad; this INV does NOT extend to them.\n- **Heuristic content matching beyond pathognomonic markers.** \"Looks Rails-ish\" is not a signature; \"contains `Rails::Application` inside `config/application.rb`\" is. The signatures must be specific enough that false positives are negligible without further evidence."  # ebe1
    fields:  # ebe1
      statement: "Framework detection must detect autoload-by-convention frameworks (Rails, Phoenix, Spring Boot, Laravel) even when both the manifest is silent and no import edges to the framework exist, provided characteristic file-structure conventions (e.g., config/application.rb containing Rails::Application) are present."  # ebe1
      root_cause: "Hypergumbo today has three detection paths: manifest scanning (handles common case), WI-lohok autoload exemption (handles Gemfile-declared autoload), WI-palol/WI-pusad import-edge promotion (handles workspace + compound-submodule imports). All three fail for the residual case where manifest is silent AND no imports exist (rare: scrubbed-manifest forks, framework-housing monorepos, pathological CI artifacts). The only remaining signal is repo file structure — distinct mechanism class from import-edge data already in the IR."  # ebe1
